        overview: "McMinn explores the complex and sometimes fraught use of Scripture in counseling. Used well, Scripture is a living, healing presence in the therapeutic encounter. Used poorly, it becomes a weapon of shame, control, or spiritual bypassing. This chapter equips counselors to use Scripture clinically, contextually, and compassionately.",
        topics: ["The difference between prescriptive and therapeutic use of Scripture","Common ways Scripture is misused in counseling contexts","Empirically supported approaches to Scripture-based interventions"],
        objectives: ["Distinguish prescriptive from therapeutic use of Scripture with case examples","Identify one common misuse of Scripture in counseling and develop a clinical response","Practice a Scripture-based intervention using the REBT-adapted model McMinn describes"],
